// integrations domain -- the read path from a browser to the integration
// registry (memql#3323).
//
// WHY A BUILTIN AND NOT A CONCEPT. Almost everything the portal shows is a
// concept row, and that is the right default. This is the case where it is
// not: which plug-ins a node registered, and whether each one's credentials
// resolved, is PROCESS STATE. It differs per replica and per node-type build
// (build tags decide which plug-ins compile in), it is recomputed at boot,
// and it is never true of "the cluster" the way a row is. Writing it into the
// graph would mean a writer, a staleness question, and a row that disagrees
// with the process it claims to describe. So it is read live, through the
// ExecuteQuery seam every other named call already rides -- no new gRPC
// message, no new transport.
//
// WHAT IT DELIBERATELY NEVER RETURNS: a credential value. The handler reports
// each credential slot's PRESENCE, its source (env / globalVariable /
// globalSecret / unset) and, where the secret store already publishes one, its
// non-reversible fingerprint. Never the value, never the ciphertext, and
// never a length. integrations/email/status.go states the invariant and
// integrations/email/status_test.go holds it: the whole reply is serialized
// and swept for planted credential values.
//
// This domain declares no concepts. That is intentional -- it contributes a
// read, not a schema.
//
// @sdk (memql#4239): the portal's Integrations page is this builtin's
// consumer, so it is on the generated client surface -- `make sdk-gen` emits
// the typed Go + TypeScript method for it. The marker widens nothing: it has
// no engine effect, the owner/admin gate lives in the handler
// (statusAuthorized), and the credential-free reply is an invariant of the
// handler, not of who may ask.

@sdk
@executor("integration.email.status")
@description("Report this node's integration registry: every plug-in the running binary registered, and for the email integration the resolved sender mode plus a slot-by-slot account of which settings and credentials are configured and where each came from. Credential VALUES are never included -- only presence, source and (for stored secrets) a fingerprint. Pass probe=true to additionally run a live, non-sending reachability check (Microsoft Graph: acquire a client-credentials token; SMTP: connect, EHLO, STARTTLS, AUTH, QUIT) and report its verdict; probe=false (the default) answers the configuration question only.")
builtin integrationStatus {
  probe  bool
}

@sdk
@executor("integration.email.configure")
@description("Set one of an integration's configuration values from an operator surface (memql#4825). OWNER OR DEVELOPER ONLY, which is stricter than reading the status report: wiring up what this cluster talks to is a developer's job, and administering people -- what an admin is for -- is a different one. Three things this does that a client calling setGlobalVariable itself cannot. The row NAME is derived from the integration's declared manifest rather than taken from the caller, so a mistyped variable cannot become a row the resolver never reads and a save that reports success and changes nothing. The row ID reuses the seeder's derivation, so the write UPDATES the row an installed cluster already has instead of adding a second row carrying the same name. And a credential is sealed server-side under the cluster master key, which exists on nodes and must never exist in a browser -- the plaintext crosses the wire once and is never sent back. Afterwards the node discards its resolved sender, so the change takes effect on its next send with no restart; other replicas pick it up on their own next send, and the reply says so rather than implying the fleet moved at once.")
builtin integrationConfigure {
  slot   string!  @description("The manifest slot to set, e.g. senderAddress or clientSecret. A slot NAME, never an environment variable: the name is stable across a rename of the variable behind it, which is the whole reason a surface keys on it.")
  value  string!  @description("The plaintext value. Sealed before storage when the slot is a credential, and never read back by any surface -- what a client can learn afterwards is that the slot is set, from where, and the command that rotates it.")
}
